Darwin: no "overdraw alpha" flags on a Metal process's draws (panvk's choice with a shader).

A34 (local to this port). panfrost_overdraw_alpha() tells the GPU, per draw, that "alpha 1 is a
full overdraw" and "alpha 0 is no overdraw". Its test reads `!info.enabled`: it looks only at the
render targets a draw does NOT write, so any draw that writes all of its targets gets both flags,
whatever its blend -- a replacing draw, an additive one, a programmable blend's -- although an
alpha of 0 still writes there. CoreAnimation draws exactly those: it clears regions of its second
attachment with a replacing draw of transparent black, and its vibrancy adds light with
premultiplied colours whose alpha is 0. panvk sets neither flag on a draw with a fragment shader.

In a process that draws through Metal (redwood_pan_metal) neither flag is set.
REDWOOD_PAN_OVERDRAW=upstream (the plug-in's rwm_overdraw=upstream) keeps the test as it is;
=fixed asks it of the targets a draw writes, as its comment says. A process that only uses
OpenGL ES keeps panfrost as upstream.

